# Self-hosting

Wyrd ships as a single server binary. It owns the durable behavior: registry writes, storage orchestration, auth, policy, audit, and the OLAP warehouse. This section is for the person running that binary.

Everything here is operational. If you want the model behind identity, tokens, and roles, read [Concepts](/concepts/). This section tells you which env vars to set, what Postgres and object storage the server needs, and what happens between process start and a server accepting requests.

## Start here

The fastest way to see the server run is local development, where it provisions its own Postgres and mints an ephemeral signing key on boot. No external services, no secrets to generate.

```bash
# From the repo root. Builds and runs wyrd-server on :8080 with
# embedded Postgres, local storage, and a dev signing key.
mise run dev:backend
```

That configuration is development-only. It is not safe for staging or production, and the server refuses to boot a production profile without a real signing key. See [Local development](/self-hosting/local-development/) for the details, then [Configuration](/self-hosting/configuration/) for the full env var surface a real deployment needs.

